---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Use Cases | BeaconGuard Regulated AI Transactions"
  description="BeaconGuard complete governed AI transaction scenarios for healthcare, financial services, and regulated internal automation with dual authorization and Verify."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Use Cases</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      BeaconGuard supports complete governed AI transactions: Request Admission
      Authorization before provider invocation, Result-to-Action Authorization
      for the declared intended enterprise action, and read-only Verify for
      decision evidence. An AI-generated result remains an untrusted proposal.
      Provider success is not enterprise authorization.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Complete transaction pattern</h2>
    <p>
      Each use case follows the same transaction shape. What changes is the
      source application, required context, declared action, and systems of
      record—not the dual-authorization model.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Enterprise application prepares a governed outbound request</li>
      <li>Request Admission Authorization returns ALLOW or DENY</li>
      <li>On ALLOW, the AI provider returns an AI-generated result</li>
      <li>Enterprise application declares the intended enterprise action</li>
      <li>Result-to-Action Authorization returns ALLOW or DENY</li>
      <li>On ALLOW, the enterprise application executes the authorized action</li>
      <li>Enterprise system of record remains authoritative for business state</li>
      <li>Verify supports investigation without granting runtime authority</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Priority transaction scenarios</h2>
    <div class="section-grid">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Healthcare operational AI</h3>
        <p>
          PHI-sensitive summarization, privacy-gated assist, and operational
          follow-up pathways where provider invocation and declared clinical or
          operational actions must be separately authorized. BeaconGuard does
          not certify clinical correctness.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/healthcare">Healthcare scenarios →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Financial crime, fraud, and dispute review</h3>
        <p>
          AML, KYC, fraud, dispute, and account-exception workflows where
          customer-impacting recommendations remain untrusted proposals until
          the declared enterprise action is authorized.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/fintech">Financial scenarios →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Regulated internal automation</h3>
        <p>
          Internal tools and agents that propose changes against production
          systems. DENY at request admission or at declared action prevents
          unauthorized enterprise effect; Verifiable Decision Records support
          governance follow-up.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/canonical-workflow">Workflow examples →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Operational patterns</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Request Admission Authorization before provider invocation</li>
      <li>
        Result-to-Action Authorization for the declared intended enterprise
        action
      </li>
      <li>Binary ALLOW or DENY at each authorization point</li>
      <li>
        Related Verifiable Decision Records for evidence, reconstruction, and
        investigation replay
      </li>
      <li>
        Fail-closed handling when required release, trust, or freshness
        conditions are not met
      </li>
      <li>Non-AI fallback owned by the enterprise application</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Regulated workflow focus</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard is focused on regulated enterprise workflows where
      authorization, evidence, and control ownership remain explicit. It is not
      a generic AI governance consulting service, chatbot, model host, or GRC
      repository.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Explore by scenario</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/healthcare">Healthcare transaction scenarios</a></li>
      <li><a href="/fintech">Financial transaction scenarios</a></li>
      <li><a href="/canonical-workflow">Complete workflow examples</a></li>
      <li><a href="/platform">Platform coverage</a></li>
      <li><a href="/verification">Verification surface</a></li>
      <li><a href="/trust-assurance">Trust Center</a></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/design-partner-program">Request Controlled Evaluation</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/contact">Start a platform discussion</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
